WEBVTT

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:28.930
<v Sean Cole>From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Sean Cole in for Ira Glass. The first punch landed on sort of the lower back right side of my skull, behind my ear. That was the one that knocked me down.
<v Sean Cole>这里是来自芝加哥WBEZ的《美国生活》节目。我是肖恩·科尔，代替艾拉·格拉斯主持。第一拳打在了我头骨右后下方，耳朵后面。那一拳把我打倒在地。

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:55.600
<v Sean Cole>This was a couple summers ago. I'd been walking down this sort of abandoned industrial block in my neighborhood in Brooklyn. It was after midnight. There was a big group of kids-- teenagers, boys-- maybe 9 or 10 of them, coming the other way. I was on the sidewalk. They were walking in the middle of the street, where the cars go. Also, I was on the phone with my friend, Sam, talking to him with my earbuds in.
<v Sean Cole>那是几个夏天前的事了。当时我正走在布鲁克林家附近一条废弃的工业街区，时间已经过了午夜。迎面走来一大群孩子——都是十几岁的男孩，大概有9到10个。我在人行道上，他们走在汽车通行的马路中间。当时我还戴着耳塞，正和我的朋友萨姆通电话。

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:24.310
<v Sean Cole>So I just kept walking and talking, glancing over at the kids a couple of times. And then I saw them see me. And they sort of stopped and said something to each other. And then a few of them, casually-- like I wouldn't notice-- started walking in my direction. I don't know if taking off in a sprint was the best decision, or if you could even call it a decision.
<v Sean Cole>于是我继续走着，聊着天，偶尔瞥那群孩子几眼。后来我看到他们发现了我，他们停下脚步，互相说了些什么。紧接着，其中几个看起来很随意——好像我不会注意到似的——开始朝我的方向走来。我不知道当时跑起来是不是最好的决定，甚至不知道那能不能算是个决定。

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:38.930
<v Sean Cole>A couple of the kids said, yeah! There are a few things I remember about the next 30 seconds or so and a few things I don't. I remember looking down at my shoes and thinking, why can't I run any faster than this?
<v Sean Cole>其中几个孩子喊道：“耶！”关于接下来的那30秒左右，我记得一些事，也有一些事我不记得了。我记得当时低头看着自己的鞋子，心想：我怎么就跑不快呢？

00:01:38.930 --> 00:02:20.400
<v Sean Cole>Sam said, what's happening? I said, I'm being chased. Who's chasing you, he said. They're chasing me, I apparently said, which I don't remember. I don't remember how I got off the ground after the first punch. I just know that a second later, I was running again and still getting punched in the head. Being punched in the head while running becomes withstandable remarkably quickly. After the second hit, my skull was like, if this is all that's going to happen, you can manage. One kid way in the back, nowhere near me, yelled, give me your wallet, but laughing, like it was a joke. They never tried to take anything. They easily could have.
<v Sean Cole>萨姆问：“怎么了？”我说：“我被人追赶。”他说：“谁在追你？”我脱口而出：“他们在追我。”但我对此毫无印象。我不记得第一拳被打倒后是怎么爬起来的，我只知道一秒钟后，我又跑了起来，头上还在挨拳头。在奔跑的同时被击打头部，适应起来快得惊人。第二次被击中后，我的头骨好像在说：如果这已经是全部，那你还能应付。最后面有个孩子，离我挺远，喊了一句：“把钱包给我”，但语气带着笑意，像是在开玩笑。他们从未试图抢走任何东西，明明他们很容易就能做到。

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:59.170
<v Sean Cole>I've tried to come up with something to compare the experience of it to, but I've never felt anything else that intensely. I've never felt as good in my life as how bad that was. I rounded the corner and saw a semi truck coming. It was the only moving vehicle around. I ran toward it with my arms straight up over my head. And when I looked back, the kids had vanished-- gone just like they'd never been there in the first place. I slowed to a walk, which is when I noticed my earbuds were neatly spooled around my left ankle, like I'd done it on purpose. One of them was smashed. And when I lifted the other one up toward my face--
<v Sean Cole>我试着找些东西来比拟这种体验，但我从未有过如此强烈的感受。我这辈子从未像那一刻那样感觉糟糕到极致。我拐过街角，看到一辆半挂车驶来，那是周围唯一的移动车辆。我双臂高举过头顶向它跑去。等我回头看时，那群孩子已经消失了——就像他们从未出现过一样。我慢下脚步，这时我才注意到耳塞整齐地缠绕在我的左脚踝上，就像我是故意那样做的一样。其中一个耳塞已经碎了，当我把另一个举到脸前时——

00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:03.000
<v Sam>Sean, are you OK? Sean. Sean. Sean, talk to me.
<v Sam>肖恩，你还好吗？肖恩。肖恩。肖恩，跟我说话。

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:04.380
<v Sean Cole>Sam was still on the phone.
<v Sean Cole>萨姆还在电话那头。

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:07.260
<v Sam>Sean. Sean?
<v Sam>肖恩。肖恩？

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:08.300
<v Sean Cole>Yeah, I'm OK.
<v Sean Cole>嗯，我没事。

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:08.760
<v Sam>Are you OK? What's up? What happened?
<v Sam>你还好吗？怎么了？发生了什么？

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:12.550
<v Sean Cole>I'm OK. I'm OK. I-- there was a gang of guys.
<v Sean Cole>我没事。我没事。我……有一群人。

00:03:12.550 --> 00:03:13.760
<v Sam>Sean, I'm recording this call--
<v Sam>肖恩，我正在录这段通话——

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:14.170
<v Sean Cole>Can you hear me?
<v Sean Cole>你能听见我说话吗？

00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:16.760
<v Sam>So tell me what happened. I'm recording the call.
<v Sam>告诉我发生了什么。我正在录音。

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:45.060
<v Sean Cole>This recording is the whole reason I'm telling you about this. When he realized something was wrong, Sam had taken the phone away from his ear and opened up this recording app, in case I needed some kind of evidence or something later. He's extremely organized that way. And I'm really glad he did it. For one thing, I have this bizarrely candid portrait of myself now, in the moment exactly after one of the worst things that's ever happened to me. It took a while for me to be able to listen to it.
<v Sean Cole>这段录音就是我告诉你们这件事的原因。当萨姆意识到出事时，他把手机从耳边拿开，打开了录音软件，以防我以后需要什么证据之类的。他在这方面非常有条理，我真的很庆幸他这么做了。一方面，我现在有了自己遭遇人生中最糟糕事件之后那一刻极其坦诚的肖像。我花了好一阵子才敢去听这段录音。

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:48.210
<v Sean Cole>But I mean, there was a lot of them. There was like a lot of guys.
<v Sean Cole>我是说，他们人很多。大概有很多家伙。

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:54.100
<v Sean Cole>I'm used to hearing my voice recorded and controlling the way I sound, like the way I'm talking to you right now.
<v Sean Cole>我习惯了听自己的录音，并控制说话的声音，就像我现在跟你说话这样。

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:54.680
<v Sam>Do you want to--
<v Sam>你想要——

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:55.420
<v Sean Cole>There were a lot of guys.
<v Sean Cole>那儿有很多家伙。

00:03:55.420 --> 00:04:04.980
<v Sean Cole>But this was like some unbidden child creature, bubbling up from my throat. When I listen to it, it's like I've finally taken some mask off that I didn't know I was wearing.
<v Sean Cole>但这听起来像是一个不请自来的儿童生物，从我的喉咙里冒出来。当我听它的时候，感觉就像我终于摘下了一个我之前都没意识到自己戴着的面具。

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:13.680
<v Sean Cole>And they kept hitting me on the head, and then I fell. And I scraped myself up a bit, but I'm OK. They didn't get anything.
<v Sean Cole>他们一直打我的头，然后我摔倒了。我擦伤了一点，但我没事。他们没拿走任何东西。

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:15.420
<v Sam>What did they hit you with?
<v Sam>他们用什么打你？

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:17.220
<v Sean Cole>I think just their hands.
<v Sean Cole>我想只是他们的手。

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:18.600
<v Sam>Oh god.
<v Sam>哦，天哪。

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:24.640
<v Sean Cole>God, that was scary. Jesus fucking Christ, that was scary.
<v Sean Cole>天啊，那太吓人了。妈的，简直吓死人了。

00:04:24.640 --> 00:04:25.440
<v Sam>But you're all right?
<v Sam>但你还好吗？

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:28.040
<v Sean Cole>Jesus. No, I'm fine. I'm OK.
<v Sean Cole>天哪。不，我很好。我没事。

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:30.040
<v Sam>Take some deep breaths, Sean. You're really going to hyperventilate.
<v Sam>做个深呼吸，肖恩。你快要过度换气了。

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:32.480
<v Sean Cole>No, I'm actually fine. Like, I--
<v Sean Cole>不，我真的没事。我是说，我——

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:48.030
<v Sean Cole>And this is another thing-- this kind of incantation I keep repeating of, I'm fine, I'm fine. Instead of telling Sam what did happen, I spend most of the call focusing on how bad it could have been. It's like I'm clinging to a buoy in the ocean after a shipwreck.
<v Sean Cole>还有一件事——我一直像念咒语一样重复说“我没事，我没事”。我没有告诉萨姆到底发生了什么，而是大部分通话时间都在强调这事儿本来可能会有多糟糕。就像海难后我紧紧抓住海面上的浮标一样。

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:51.090
<v Sean Cole>It could have been a lot worse.
<v Sean Cole>本来可能更糟糕的。

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:52.470
<v Sam>Jesus.
<v Sam>天哪。

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:55.610
<v Sean Cole>It could have been a lot worse. I lost my glasses.
<v Sean Cole>本来可能更糟糕的。我把眼镜弄丢了。

00:04:55.610 --> 00:04:56.670
<v Sam>Oh no.
<v Sam>哦，不。

00:04:56.670 --> 00:05:15.740
<v Sean Cole>But it's OK. I mean, I'm fine. I'm OK. I'm OK. It could have been way worse. I'm actually fine. I'm actually fine.
<v Sean Cole>但没关系。我是说，我很好。我没事。我没事。本来可能更糟糕的。我真的没事。我真的没事。

00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:45.390
<v Sean Cole>I never say things could be worse. Almost every other time in my life has been better than this one. And yet most of the time, I just brood about what's going wrong or what I wish I'd done differently. But here, I was clearly just paving over all the broken glass of what happened to me immediately, counting my blessings in a way I'm never usually able to. And of course, it could have been way worse. Truly catastrophic things happen to people all over the world every day.
<v Sean Cole>我从不说“本来可能更糟糕”这种话。我生命中几乎每一个其他时刻都比这一刻好。然而大多数时候，我只是苦思冥想哪里出了问题，或者希望自己能有不同的做法。但在这里，我显然是在掩盖自己遭遇的所有创伤碎片，以一种我通常无法做到的方式数算着自己的恩典。当然，本来可能会糟糕得多。世界各地每天都在发生真正灾难性的事情。

00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:53.210
<v Sean Cole>I remember talking to Sam for 45 minutes. It was actually about 12 minutes, which I couldn't believe. I was like, where's the rest of it?
<v Sean Cole>我记得跟萨姆聊了45分钟。实际上大概只有12分钟，我简直不敢相信。我当时想，剩下那些哪去了？

00:05:53.210 --> 00:06:17.710
<v Sean Cole>By minute six, six minutes after the attack, we're already starting to laugh about stuff. I told him that I had been heading down to my car to get a pack of cigarettes. And he says, yeah, well, I guess it'll kill you. It's good to know we were able to talk about it like that so quickly. It makes a lot of bad things seem more survivable.
<v Sean Cole>到了第六分钟，也就是袭击发生六分钟后，我们已经开始拿这件事开玩笑了。我告诉他，我本来要去车里拿包香烟。他说：“是啊，好吧，我猜那烟会杀了你。”很高兴知道我们能这么快就以这种方式谈论它。这让很多糟糕的事情看起来更容易承受了。

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:47.740
<v Sean Cole>We spend so much time trying to control how we're seen by other people. But sometimes we can catch an unexpected glimpse of ourselves that we haven't manicured and that has way more information in it about how we actually are. It might not be the funnest thing to witness, but at least we know. In today's show, we got two stories in which that happens-- two different people seeing themselves in the wild and what it confirms, or doesn't, about who they think they are. Stay with us.
<v Sean Cole>我们花太多时间试图控制自己在别人眼中的形象。但有时我们可以捕捉到自己意想不到的一面，那些未加修饰、包含了我们真实状态信息的自我。 witness 它可能不是最愉快的，但至少我们了解了。在今天的节目里，我们有两个故事发生过类似的情况——两个人以一种野生的方式看到了自己，这证实了或者否定了他们对自己身份的认知。请继续收听。

00:06:47.740 --> 00:07:04.410
<v Sean Cole>Jesus. And that was something. That'll wake you up, won't it? Jesus.
<v Sean Cole>天哪。那真是……那会让你清醒过来，对吧？天哪。

00:07:04.410 --> 00:07:24.860
<v Sean Cole>Act I. You don't have to be a star to be in my show. I want to tell you about my friend Zack's first big break-- Zack McDermott. We're pretty different people. Zack's from Wichita, and he has this kind of macho Kansan self certainty about him on the surface. I do an impression of him that I finally broke out when we were hanging with each other a couple of months ago.
<v Sean Cole>第一幕。你不必成为明星也能出现在我的秀里。我想告诉你关于我朋友扎克·麦克德莫特的第一次重大突破。我们是非常不同的人。扎克来自威奇托，他表面上有一种堪萨斯男子汉式的自我笃定。几个月前我们聚在一起时，我模仿了他的样子，他对此深表赞同。

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:33.500
<v Sean Cole>You know, I'm a writer. I wasn't able to get gussied up for you. I wasn't expecting you to be looking at my hair like that.
<v Sean Cole>你知道，我是一个作家。我没能为你打扮得光鲜亮丽。我没想到你会盯着我的头发看。

00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:36.290
<v Zack McDermott>That was pretty good. That flatters me a great deal.
<v Zack McDermott>那模仿得真不错。这让我非常受宠若惊。

00:07:36.290 --> 00:07:38.150
<v Sean Cole>Why?
<v Sean Cole>为什么？

00:07:38.150 --> 00:07:43.690
<v Zack McDermott>I don't think you usually do impressions of people, unless you kind of really like them or don't like them at all.
<v Zack McDermott>我不认为你通常会模仿别人，除非你真的很喜欢他们，或者根本不喜欢他们。

00:07:43.690 --> 00:07:45.080
<v Sean Cole>Or if you super don't like them.
<v Sean Cole>或者你超级不喜欢他们。

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:45.340
<v Zack McDermott>Right.
<v Zack McDermott>对。

00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:45.680
<v Sean Cole>Yeah.
<v Sean Cole>是啊。

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:47.570
<v Zack McDermott>And I feel like you more super like me than super don't.
<v Zack McDermott>我觉得你更倾向于超级喜欢我，而不是超级不喜欢。

00:07:47.570 --> 00:07:49.170
<v Sean Cole>I super like you.
<v Sean Cole>我超级喜欢你。

00:07:49.170 --> 00:08:05.480
<v Sean Cole>Zack is this singular combination of things I really haven't encountered before. As well as being swaggery, he can also be awkward and vulnerable. He's conspicuously caring for the people he cares about. After my assault, he made me promise I'd call him in the middle of the night if I needed to.
<v Sean Cole>扎克是我从未遇到过的一种独特的结合体。除了傲慢之外，他也可以表现得笨拙且脆弱。他明显地关心他所爱的人。在我遇袭后，他让我保证如果需要的话，半夜也要给他打电话。

00:08:05.480 --> 00:08:29.380
<v Sean Cole>He spent about seven years working as a public defender for the Legal Aid Society of New York, sometimes juggling more than 60 cases, righteously indignant about the court system. He's always giving advice, but he also needs to be reassured a lot. And he really likes other people's attention. He's always watching himself, in ways both literal and not, which, anyway, brings me to the story of the big break.
<v Sean Cole>他曾作为纽约法律援助协会的公设辩护律师工作了大约七年，有时同时处理60多起案件，对司法系统义愤填膺。他总是给别人建议，但也需要别人给他很多心理安慰。他真的很喜欢别人的关注。他总是以各种方式注视着自己，无论是字面意义还是其他意义，无论如何，这让我想到了他那个重大突破的故事。

00:08:29.380 --> 00:08:49.310
<v Sean Cole>See, about 10 years ago, when he was still a public defender, Zack was also trying to make it as a comedian, grinding away, sometimes doing four or five open mics a night. His act was pretty intense back then. He had a mohawk, and Frank Zappa mustache, and jazz patch. One time, he came out onstage, just shouting lyrics from the rapper, Gucci Mane.
<v Sean Cole>大约10年前，当他还是公设辩护律师时，扎克也试图成为一名喜剧演员，努力打拼，有时一晚要参加四五场公开麦。那时他的表演非常狂野。他留着莫霍克发型，蓄着弗兰克·扎帕式的小胡子，还有爵士补丁。有一次，他上台时只是在大声念唱说唱歌手Gucci Mane的歌词。

00:08:49.310 --> 00:08:57.070
<v Zack McDermott>[RAPPING]
<v Zack McDermott>[说唱音乐]

00:08:57.070 --> 00:09:17.090
<v Sean Cole>He'd usually record his set, just with a little video camera or phone. And he also had this friend who was always egging him on, sort of repping him in a way. Zack always refers to him just as the producer. He doesn't want me to say who the guy is, but he's famous adjacent-- ran with a really heavy crowd. And apparently, he had big dreams for Zack's future.
<v Sean Cole>他通常会录下他的演出，就用小型摄像机或手机。他还有个朋友一直在怂恿他，某种程度上算是他的经纪人。扎克总是只称呼他为“制作人”。他不想让我说出那是谁，但他和名人圈子挨得很近——混迹于一个非常强势的群体。显然，他对比扎克的未来有着宏大的梦想。

00:09:17.090 --> 00:09:22.220
<v Zack McDermott>It was like, oh, yeah, don't worry. I know Hova. Don't worry, I know Jimmy Fallon. Don't worry, I know--
<v Zack McDermott>就像，哦，是的，别担心。我认识Hova（Jay-Z）。别担心，我认识吉米·法伦。别担心，我认识——

00:09:22.220 --> 00:09:23.360
<v Sean Cole>Hova is Jay-Z.
<v Sean Cole>Hova就是Jay-Z。

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:40.650
<v Zack McDermott>Yeah. You know? Meeting all these people with him and him telling me, like, you're my project, man. I'm putting everything behind you. I'm willing to put all my money behind you. I'm willing to put all my connects behind you. You're next, you know? Trust me. When I push the button, it's going to go fast.
<v Zack McDermott>是啊，你知道吗？和他见了很多人，他告诉我就像，“你是我的项目，伙计。我把一切都押在你身上。我愿意把所有的钱都投在你身上。我愿意动用我所有的关系。下一个红的就是你，你知道吗？相信我。当我按下按钮时，一切都会飞速发展。”

00:09:40.650 --> 00:09:57.590
<v Sean Cole>They worked on a pilot for a TV show together, held a casting call for the role of Zack's girlfriend, collected headshots, did screen tests-- the whole thing. And then, in the fall of 2009, Zack found himself walking out of his apartment building and onto the set of a TV pilot in which he was the star.
<v Sean Cole>他们一起制作了一个电视节目的试播集，为扎克女朋友的角色进行了选角，收集了头像，进行了试镜——全套流程。然后，在2009年的秋天，扎克发现自己走出公寓大楼，走进了他作为明星主演的一部电视试播集的片场。

00:09:57.590 --> 00:10:19.910
<v Zack McDermott>Everything just looked perfect, like a Coke commercial. It was a gorgeous day. It was a fall day in New York-- sunny. There was a realistic amount of people on the street, but not so many that the scene is going to look like rush hour. Because it wasn't. It was the middle of the day. It should look like the middle of the day. And everyone is also just a little too attractive.
<v Zack McDermott>一切看起来都很完美，就像可口可乐的广告。那是美好的一天。纽约的一个秋日——阳光明媚。街上的人流适中，不会多到让场景看起来像高峰时段。因为并不是高峰期。那是在中午。看起来就应该是中午的样子。每个人看起来也都有点太有吸引力了。

00:10:19.910 --> 00:10:20.980
<v Sean Cole>Like actors.
<v Sean Cole>就像演员一样。

00:10:20.980 --> 00:10:22.370
<v Zack McDermott>Everyone.
<v Zack McDermott>每个人都是。

00:10:22.370 --> 00:10:41.270
<v Sean Cole>I don't have audio of this because it was never actually recorded. Because it wasn't a TV pilot. Those people on the street weren't actors. This wasn't Zack's first big break into show business. It was his first big psychotic break, like the part of his brain that wanted to be in the spotlight all the time had suddenly exploded.
<v Sean Cole>我没有这段音频，因为实际上根本没有被录下来。因为那不是什么电视试播集。街上的那些人不是演员。这也不是扎克在演艺圈的第一次重大突破。这是他第一次严重的精神崩溃，就像他大脑里那个一直渴望成为焦点的一部分突然爆炸了。

00:10:41.270 --> 00:10:59.570
<v Sean Cole>Zack was experiencing a type of delusion that we've talked about a little bit on the show before. It's called the Truman Show delusion-- not incredibly common, but not as rare as you might think either. Sufferers of Truman Show delusion basically think they're being filmed all the time by hidden cameras, just like the Jim Carrey character in the movie The Truman Show.
<v Sean Cole>扎克经历的是一种我们节目之前提到过的妄想。它被称为“楚门秀妄想”——虽然不太普遍，但也没有你想象的那么罕见。楚门秀妄想的患者基本认为他们一直被隐藏的摄像机拍摄，就像电影《楚门秀》里的金·凯瑞角色一样。

00:10:59.570 --> 00:11:10.370
<v Sean Cole>In the weeks leading up to the break, Zack had been showing signs of this oncoming split with reality-- little harbingers and then bigger ones. He'd been growing more and more manic, sleeping less and less.
<v Sean Cole>在崩溃前的几周，扎克已经表现出这种与现实分裂的迹象——一些小预兆，然后是更大的预兆。他变得越来越狂躁，睡眠越来越少。

00:11:10.370 --> 00:11:26.540
<v Zack McDermott>I had some friends and family start to be concerned about me. And I kind of just brushed it all off. You don't understand what's going on in my brain. You don't understand how to write comedy. You don't understand how to do comedy. You don't understand how to do comedy after you're a lawyer all day.
<v Zack McDermott>我的一些朋友和家人开始为我担心。我基本上都把他们打发了。你们不明白我的大脑里在发生什么。你们不明白怎么写喜剧。你们不明白怎么做喜剧。你们不明白在做了一整天律师后怎么做喜剧。

00:11:26.540 --> 00:11:32.990
<v Zack McDermott>You don't understand how to write a joke. Look, it's a mathematical equation. Let me show you. This is a premise. This is why this is funny. This is why that's not funny.
<v Zack McDermott>你不明白怎么写笑话。看，这是一个数学公式。让我展示给你看。这是前提。这就是为什么这个好笑。这就是为什么那个不好笑。

00:11:32.990 --> 00:11:34.070
<v Sean Cole>That's your inner monologue.
<v Sean Cole>那是你的内心独白。

00:11:34.070 --> 00:11:36.530
<v Zack McDermott>You don't understand-- and outer monologue.
<v Zack McDermott>你不明白——还有外在独白。

00:11:36.530 --> 00:11:56.600
<v Sean Cole>Even the producer was concerned. He told Zack he had to go see a doctor if they were going to keep working together. And yet, when Zack walked out of his apartment building onto the street that day, he was sure the producer had staged everything just for him. And Zack was ready to perform, thinking of himself as some hybrid of Ali G and Johnny Knoxville.
<v Sean Cole>甚至那位制作人也很担心。他告诉扎克，如果他们还要继续合作，他就必须去看医生。然而，当那天扎克走出公寓楼来到街上时，他确信制作人已经为他安排好了一切。扎克准备好表演了，把自己想象成阿里·G和强尼·诺克斯维尔的结合体。

00:11:56.600 --> 00:12:25.840
<v Zack McDermott>I'm thinking my friend, the brilliant producer, had decided, you know what? Zack's not really an actor. He's not going to know how to act. I'm just going to throw him in somewhere. He'll eventually get what he's supposed to be doing and he'll just kind of like, basically method act himself. He could probably do that. You know? So, I go to Tompkins Square Park. Soon as I'm in there, I spot generic old man on park bench.
<v Zack McDermott>我以为我的朋友，那位天才制作人决定，你知道吗？扎克不是真正的演员。他不知道怎么演。我就把他随便丢进某个地方。他最终会明白他该做什么，他会进行沉浸式表演。他或许能做到。你知道吗？所以我去了汤普金斯广场公园。一进公园，我就发现了长椅上的典型老头。

00:12:25.840 --> 00:12:28.280
<v Sean Cole>That's what it would say if he was in the credits.
<v Sean Cole>如果他在演职员表里，那就是这么写的。

00:12:28.280 --> 00:12:32.860
<v Zack McDermott>Yes, and he had a bike. And he was too old to have a bike.
<v Zack McDermott>是的，他还有一辆自行车。他太老了，不该骑自行车。

00:12:32.860 --> 00:12:59.250
<v Sean Cole>Another tell that the entire neighborhood had been cordoned off to showcase Zack's comedic talents. Zack thought it would be funny if he grabbed generic old man's bike and did a couple laps around the park with it. Generic old man did not take kindly to Zack trying to grab his bike away. Zack relented. It was surely a sign from the producer. Keep things moving. So Zack takes off in a sprint toward the middle of the park and vaults over the wall of this big doggy playpen.
<v Sean Cole>这是整个街区被戒严以展示扎克喜剧天赋的另一个暗示。扎克觉得如果他抢走那个老头的自行车，在公园里骑上两圈会很有趣。典型老头并不乐意扎克抢他的自行车。扎克放弃了。这肯定来自制作人的暗示。继续前进。于是扎克朝着公园中间冲刺，越过了狗乐园的围栏。

00:12:59.250 --> 00:13:11.720
<v Zack McDermott>I get down, like, on all fours myself. And I gallop with the pack. Running toward the end of the fence, I jump out the other side. And I'm like, all right, cool. Whatever that was worth, I did it. You know?
<v Zack McDermott>我自己趴在地上。我和这群狗一起奔跑。跑向围栏尽头时，我跳到了另一边。我心想，好极了，不管这值不值得，反正我做到了。你知道吗？

00:13:11.720 --> 00:13:13.240
<v Sean Cole>That was funny, maybe.
<v Sean Cole>那或许很有趣。

00:13:13.240 --> 00:13:31.840
<v Zack McDermott>Yeah, that was funny maybe. Who knows? And I also was thinking that the pedestrians were extras, doubling as production assistants. And I'm supposed to follow them, follow the foot traffic that will take you where you need to go. No one's coming up to me with a clipboard and a shot list or anything, right?
<v Zack McDermott>是的，或许有趣。谁知道呢？我还以为行人们是群众演员，同时也兼任制作助理。我应该跟随他们，跟着人流走，他们会带我去我该去的地方。没有人带着剪贴板和拍摄清单来找我，对吧？

00:13:31.840 --> 00:13:44.320
<v Zack McDermott>So they're then phoning ahead to the producer or whatever production unit, saying, OK, he's on First Avenue. He's almost to Houston. And like, OK, we'll make him go west then.
<v Zack McDermott>所以他们会提前打电话给制作人或制作单位，说：“好了，他在第一大道。快到休斯顿街了。”然后就说：“好吧，那我们就让他往西走。”

00:13:44.320 --> 00:14:12.220
<v Sean Cole>Whenever Zack tells this story, he has a whole menu of deranged things he did that day to talk about-- running around the soccer field with his shorts pulled down while a game is going on, ordering champagne on a hotel patio and proceeding to yell at the cameras in the trees. As Zack remembers it-- and Zack's memory is all we have to go on-- this television non-debut lasted for about 10 hours-- a 10 hour narcissism variety show arranged for him to give the performance of his life.
<v Sean Cole>每当扎克讲起这个故事，他总有一整套在那天做的疯狂事迹可说——把裤子脱了在足球场周围乱跑，在酒店露台上点香槟，然后对着树里的摄像机大喊大叫。正如扎克所记忆的那样——而扎克的记忆是我们唯一能依靠的东西——这场电视非首秀持续了大约10个小时——一场为他安排的10小时自恋综艺节目，让他献出了人生中最精彩的表演。

00:14:12.220 --> 00:14:18.220
<v Zack McDermott>I sprinted across the intersection of-- I think it was Houston and First Avenue.
<v Zack McDermott>我冲过了十字路口——我想是休斯顿和第一大道。

00:14:18.220 --> 00:14:19.300
<v Sean Cole>While cars were moving?
<v Sean Cole>当汽车在行驶时？

00:14:19.300 --> 00:14:20.200
<v Zack McDermott>Oh, yeah.
<v Zack McDermott>噢，是的。

00:14:20.200 --> 00:14:23.230
<v Sean Cole>That's incredibly dangerous.
<v Sean Cole>那极其危险。

00:14:23.230 --> 00:14:26.710
<v Zack McDermott>Not if it's professional drivers on a closed course.
<v Zack McDermott>如果是在封闭赛道上有专业车手的话，那就不危险了。

00:14:26.710 --> 00:14:42.640
<v Sean Cole>At its height, everything he did was the right thing to be doing. He was a master of improvisation, following all the cues perfectly. And then he started getting tired and then exhausted. Everything was still a clue, but he wasn't sure what any of it meant anymore.
<v Sean Cole>在他妄想的巅峰期，他做的一切都是正确的。他是即兴表演的大师，完美地跟随所有的暗示。然后他开始感到疲倦，接着筋疲力尽。一切仍然是线索，但他不再确定这些意味着什么了。

00:14:42.640 --> 00:15:00.190
<v Sean Cole>Somewhere along the way, he lost his shirt and also his shoes. And the next thing he knew, he was standing on a Brooklyn subway platform, hands behind his head like a captured soldier, crying so hard, he flushed his contacts out of his eyes. All he wanted was for someone to yell, cut, thinking to himself--
<v Sean Cole>在这个过程中，他弄丢了衬衫，也丢了鞋子。接着他发现自己站在布鲁克林的地铁站台上，双手抱头，像个被俘的士兵一样大哭，哭得太厉害，把隐形眼镜都冲出了眼眶。他只想要有人喊“卡”，心里想着——

00:15:00.190 --> 00:15:12.130
<v Zack McDermott>OK, fine, you've got your shots. Can't you see that I'm bawling? Can't you see that I'm here suffering? Can't you see that I'm done? Like, come out, come out, wherever you are, Mr. Producer.
<v Zack McDermott>好吧，够了，你们已经拍够素材了。你们看不出我正在嚎啕大哭吗？看不出我正痛苦不堪吗？看不出我受够了吗？出来吧，出来吧，不管你在哪，制作人先生。

00:15:12.130 --> 00:15:30.340
<v Zack McDermott>So I'm crying. And I just scream, what do you want from me? Two cops come up. Their uniforms look real-- super real.
<v Zack McDermott>所以我大哭。我大喊：“你们到底想从我这儿得到什么？”两个警察走过来。他们的制服看起来是真的——超级真。

00:15:30.340 --> 00:15:37.230
<v Zack McDermott>The next thing I recall with any clarity is being in the back of an ambulance.
<v Zack McDermott>我能清晰记得的下一件事就是躺在救护车后面。

00:15:37.230 --> 00:15:38.530
<v Sean Cole>Huh.
<v Sean Cole>哼。

00:15:38.530 --> 00:15:49.120
<v Zack McDermott>And eventually, I hear a radio crack on. It's like, psh. Intake available at Bellevue Psych Ward.
<v Zack McDermott>最后，我听到无线电响了。就像是，“滋——贝尔维尤精神病院有接收名额。”

00:15:49.120 --> 00:16:20.170
<v Sean Cole>Zack was eventually diagnosed with bipolar disorder type I, which can involve psychosis sometimes. In Zack's case, lack of sleep and daily pot smoking might have triggered the episode. At Bellevue, he says they dosed him up with antipsychotics. At times, he could barely lift his chin off of his chest. He drooled a lot. He couldn't go outside. Zack's only experience with psych wards before this was as a public defender, representing EDPs, or emotionally disturbed people. And now he was one.
<v Sean Cole>扎克最终被诊断为I型双相障碍，有时会伴有精神病症状。在扎克的案例中，缺乏睡眠和每天吸食大麻可能诱发了这次发作。在贝尔维尤，他说他们给他服用了抗精神病药物。有时他甚至无法把下巴从胸口抬起来。他流了很多口水。他不能去外面。扎克在此之前接触精神病院的唯一经历是作为公设辩护律师，代理EDP，也就是情感障碍患者。现在他自己也成了其中一员。

00:16:20.170 --> 00:16:37.790
<v Sean Cole>The delusion that he was on TV lasted in one form or another for the next 10 days. When he saw his mother, Cindy, coming down the hall on day two, he wasn't sure at first if it was her or an actor in prosthetics. He calls her bird because of the way she snaps her head back and forth sometimes.
<v Sean Cole>关于他在电视上的妄想在某种程度上持续了接下来的10天。当他在第二天看到他的母亲辛迪走过大厅时，他起初不确定那是她本人还是戴着假体的演员。他叫她“鸟”，因为她有时前后猛甩头的方式。

00:16:37.790 --> 00:16:54.320
<v Sean Cole>The bird is here, she told him. The bird can't be here, he said. The bird lives in Wichita. She said, the bird got on a plane.
<v Sean Cole>“鸟来了，”她告诉他。“鸟不可能在这儿，”他说，“鸟住在威奇托。”她说，“鸟坐了飞机过来。”

00:16:54.320 --> 00:17:28.340
<v Sean Cole>It took more than a year and a couple more episodes like this before he got stable. Along the way, he figured out a few things. He had to be good about taking his meds, and sleep was key. He couldn't burn the candle at all ends, like he had been, going on stage all the time. So he stopped, for the most part. He kept working his day job as a lawyer. They say you have to be at least a little crazy to be a comedian. But it's also possible to be so crazy that you can't be a comedian.
<v Sean Cole>在他稳定下来之前，他经历了超过一年和几次类似的发作。在这一过程中，他明白了一些事。他必须按时服药，睡眠是关键。他不能像以前那样整天熬夜，频繁上台表演。所以他大部分时间停止了。他继续做着律师的日常工作。人们常说喜剧演员必须得有一点疯狂。但也可能疯狂到根本无法成为喜剧演员。

00:17:28.340 --> 00:18:02.610
<v Sean Cole>Zack has told the story of the psychotic break countless times at this point. It's a good romp, and he's got it down-- all the different parts. He tells it entertainingly. But while it's one thing to recount something like that from memory, it's another thing if someone catches it on video. You can actually see yourself in psychosis. I say this because after Zack got better and thought he was totally out of the woods, sanity wise, he did have another psychotic break. And it was captured on video. And for the first time in his life, sane Zack would be able to see himself in the midst of Truman Show delusion.
<v Sean Cole>扎克已经无数次讲过这次精神崩溃的故事了。这确实是一场不错的闹剧，他掌握得很好——所有不同的部分。他讲得很有趣。但虽然凭记忆讲述那样的事情是一回事，如果有视频录下来又是另一回事。你真的可以看到自己处于精神病状态。我之所以这么说，是因为扎克康复后，以为自己完全走出了困境，但在理智方面，他又一次精神崩溃了。而且这次被视频拍下来了。这是他人生中第一次，理智的扎克能够看到自己在楚门秀妄想中的样子。

00:18:02.610 --> 00:18:04.460
<v Woman>Do you need to call the cops, or is he OK?
<v Woman>你需要报警吗，还是他没事？

00:18:04.460 --> 00:18:04.920
<v Man>Yeah.
<v Man>是的。

00:18:04.920 --> 00:18:06.310
<v Zach McDermott>Shut the fuck up!
<v Zach McDermott>闭嘴！

00:18:06.310 --> 00:18:36.110
<v Sean Cole>Actually, let me-- sorry, I'm just going to stop that and explain what happened. So after a while, Zack quit lawyering and decided to try to make it as a writer. He wrote a book about that first psychotic break and his disorder, called Gorilla and the Bird. Gorilla is Zack's nickname because he's really hairy and barrel chested. Again, the bird is his mom. It was optioned for a TV series before it even came out. HBO is planning to run it. The director Jean-Marc Vallée, who did Big Little Lies and Sharp Objects, is on board.
<v Sean Cole>实际上，让我——抱歉，我先停一下，解释一下发生了什么。在一段时间后，扎克辞去了律师工作，决定尝试成为一名作家。他写了一本关于那次精神崩溃和他的障碍的书，叫《大猩猩与鸟》。大猩猩是扎克的绰号，因为他体毛很重，胸部宽阔。还是那句话，鸟是他的妈妈。这本书还没出版就被电视连续剧买下了改编权。HBO正计划制作。执导了《大小谎言》和《利器》的导演让-马克·瓦雷已经加入了。

00:18:36.110 --> 00:18:58.760
<v Sean Cole>Zack and I have had entire conversations about who might play him on the TV show about the time he thought he was on a TV show and wasn't. In fact, his therapist said to him-- sort of joking, but sort of not-- you know, you might end up playing yourself on that show. To which Zack basically responded, yeah, I think they might want to go with one of these A-list celebrities they're considering.
<v Sean Cole>扎克和我进行过完整的对话，讨论谁可能在关于他以为自己在电视上、但其实并不在那里的那段经历的电视剧中扮演他。事实上，他的治疗师曾开玩笑说——半开玩笑半认真——“你知道，你可能会最终在那个节目里扮演你自己。”扎克基本上回应说：“是啊，我想他们可能想选那些他们正在考虑的一线明星。”

00:18:58.760 --> 00:19:16.460
<v Sean Cole>And then, Zack was trying to figure out ways to promote his book. And of course, these days, a lot of authors make videos when their books come out. And so Zack hired a production crew to shoot footage for-- he wasn't sure what. A promotional video, but if it was any good, maybe they'd do a bigger documentary.
<v Sean Cole>然后，扎克在想办法推广他的书。当然，现在的作者在新书出版时都会拍视频。所以扎克聘请了一个制作团队来拍摄素材——他也不确定拍什么。一个宣传视频，但如果效果好，或许他们会做一个更大的纪录片。

00:19:16.460 --> 00:19:40.920
<v Sean Cole>He wanted to take them back home with him to Wichita to interview some of the people that he'd written about in his memoir, meaning his family, and have the crew follow him around his hometown for a week, just rolling on everything, documentary style. The crew consisted of two professional cameramen and a very seasoned TV producer that Zack happened to be friends with. He'd been around the cameras a bunch before this, by the way. And it didn't trigger his disorder.
<v Sean Cole>他想带他们回威奇托老家采访他书里写到的一些人，也就是他的家人，让摄制组跟着他在家乡拍摄一周，用纪录片的方式记录一切。摄制组由两名专业摄影师和一位扎克恰好是朋友的资深电视制作人组成。顺便说一句，他在这次之前已经接触过很多次摄像机了。这并没有触发他的障碍。

00:19:40.920 --> 00:20:17.390
<v Sean Cole>So the morning the delusion really hit, Zack hadn't met up with the film crew yet. They were actually wondering where he was. He was supposed to call. Instead, he was driving around Wichita by himself in his grandfather's pickup. He was fighting serious back pain. And he had barely slept in three days. Again, sleeplessness is a huge trigger for his episodes. In the book, he writes, "The solution to mania is so simple, yet so hard to come by. Just sleep." And the thing his therapist said came back to him-- that Jean-Marc Vallée might want him to play himself on the TV show.
<v Sean Cole>所以妄想真正发作的那天早上，扎克还没有见到摄制组。他们其实还在纳闷他在哪儿。他本该打电话的。相反，他正独自开着他祖父的皮卡车在威奇托四处转悠。他忍受着严重的背痛。而且他已经三天几乎没睡了。同样，睡眠不足是他发作的巨大诱因。他在书里写道：“狂躁的解药很简单，却很难得到。只是睡觉。”他治疗师说的话浮现在他脑海里——让-马克·瓦雷可能希望他在电视剧里扮演他自己。

00:20:17.390 --> 00:20:28.680
<v Zack McDermott>I came to believe that, oh my god, maybe my therapist was contacted by whoever. And maybe I really am auditioning for myself. So--
<v Zack McDermott>我开始相信，天哪，也许我的治疗师被某人联系过了。也许我真的在为我自己试镜。所以——

00:20:28.680 --> 00:20:35.520
<v Sean Cole>Right now. Like, I am auditioning to play the role of myself. I'm in the process of an audition.
<v Sean Cole>就在此时此刻。就像，我正在试镜扮演我自己。我正处于试镜的过程中。

00:20:35.520 --> 00:20:44.560
<v Zack McDermott>Yes. And I was just testing it slowly because I was like, be careful, you know? You've had this delusion before, and you've been wrong.
<v Zack McDermott>是的。我只是在慢慢测试，因为我当时想，小心点，你知道的？你以前有过这种妄想，结果是你错了。

